`Maintenance mode`, `Service name`, `API key`, `Upload`, `Migration`, and three design-gallery page
titles branded "NetScript design system" (one describing "the scaffolded @netscript/fresh-ui
theme"). The landing page's stat row measured the app: "UI source: app-owned", "Theme: paper & ink",
"see RECORD.md".

A denylist only catches what someone already thought to name, which is why this survived three
passes under a green R8. The terms are now in the test alongside two patterns for the _shape_ of the
fiction — naming the UI registry a component was copied from, and prose pointing at the repository
record. The registry pattern caught a fourth site the hand-sweep missed.

## The landing page carries no stat row

**Found 2026-09-04 (design steer #2, pass 9).** `HomeView` receives no billing data, so the four
cards that filled its `StatsGrid` could only describe the app that was rendering them. Deleted
rather than rewritten (`57085f8`): a card that counts the app is a card the customer cannot read,
and the lede plus the route cards already do the page's job. `appName` went with it, so
`routes/index.tsx` no longer passes it. If the landing page ever gets a real number — open runs,
collected this period — it should come back as a `WidgetCard` with a hero, on data this layer
actually has.
